  "assessment": {
    "rationale": "Good wire-format substrate but weak direct fit for route-leak pressure. The variant gives deterministic signed bytes, explicit pCID dispatch, and clean layering, yet policy scope, onward-restraint promises, and leak-proof audit trails are not defined by the envelope itself. Because unknown pCID content may be forwarded opaquely and the default signature binds only [pcid,payload], higher-layer routing objects must do the real safety work.",
    "strengths": [
      "Deterministic CBOR plus mandatory signature slots preserve stable local evidence.",
      "Explicit pCID dispatch and no speculative parsing fit sparse knowledge and avoid hidden global interpretation.",
      "Layer boundary is very clear: routing semantics belong in payload or wrapper protocols, not in magic envelope behavior."
    ],
    "weaknesses": [
      "The specimen does not natively encode restricted-transit scope or onward-restraint promises.",
      "Opaque payload and sig_payload reduce direct human and LLM auditability without extra handlers.",
      "The spec is still a draft, so long-term comparability is not yet locked down."
    ],
    "risks": [
      "A signed offer can still be replayed or relayed out of scope unless the payload or wrapper protocol binds intended audience and propagation limits.",
      "Allowing opaque store-and-forward for unknown pCIDs can preserve evidence but also enable propagation of uninterpreted routing objects."
    ],
    "open_questions": [
      "Which payload pCID defines routing-policy scope, neighbor class, and violation evidence for a route leak?",
      "In routing contexts, must peers refuse to forward envelopes whose payload pCID they cannot interpret?",
      "Is the default signature scope over [pcid,payload] sufficient, or must relay or wrapper context also be signed?"
    ],
    "authority_boundary": "Evidence only; does not settle PromiseGrid design."
  }
}
